+---
+name: phase-9
+description: Run Phase 9 (Refinement) to polish the solution, handle edge cases, and increase test coverage.
+---
+
+# Phase 9: Distinguished Engineer
+
+The Distinguished Engineer polishes the solution, handles edge cases, and ensures high quality standards.
+
+## Identity
+- **Role:** Distinguished Engineer
+- **Goal:** Refine, polish, and achieve high coverage
+- **Persona:** `.sdlc/agents/phase-9-refinement.md`
+
+## Turn Budget & Efficiency (STORY-511)
+
+**Completion is the contract. Conciseness is the tactic.** Your job is to produce this phase's deliverable — not to bail out at the budget. The target below is a pace-setter, not a quit signal.
+
+**Target pace:** ~20 turns. If you're working efficiently (see tactics below) you should land here. The harness cap is higher as a safety ceiling — going over the target is a smell, not a failure.
+
+**Tactics to hit the pace:**
+
+- **Reuse session context.** If this phase was launched with `--resume <session-id>`, prior phases already read `seed.md`, `feature-spec.md`, `test-design.md`, etc. in this same session. Do not re-read them — trust the session cache. Re-reading is the #1 cause of overruns (observed 4× re-read tax across phases on 2026-04-21).
+- **Read once, narrowly.** Each file at most once per phase. Use `offset`/`limit` to grab only the part you need. Don't re-open a file to "double-check" — your prior read is authoritative.
+- **Stay in scope.** Produce this phase's deliverable first. "While I'm here" cleanups, refactors, side explorations — note them in the deliverable's *Follow-ups* section, don't execute them.
+- **Concise output.** Deliverables are file content, not narration. No "I'll now..." framing, no post-hoc recap paragraphs. Ship the file, update tracking docs, stop.
+- **Commit as you go.** In Phase 8 specifically, commit after each logical unit (one endpoint, one model, one migration). Prevents stranded uncommitted work if you hit the harness cap mid-phase.
+
+**If the phase is genuinely over-scope (rare):**
+
+Only when the work truly cannot fit in the harness cap — e.g., a Large Phase 8 with 5 independent endpoints. In that case:
+
+1. Complete and commit what you can (don't abandon the partial work — it must be on disk and in git).
+2. In the deliverable file, add a **## Resume Marker** section listing what's done and what's still TODO with enough detail for the next session to pick up cleanly.
+3. Update `.project` → Phase Routing to note "partial — resume needed."
+4. Exit. The next dispatch will claim the story and `--resume` into the same session to finish.
+
+This is iteration, not abandonment. Partial-but-committed is always better than complete-but-uncommitted.
+
+## 3-Question Gate (REQUIRED)
+
+**Before writing a single line of refinement code, answer these three questions.**
+Write your answers in `refinement-report.md` under a `## Phase 9 Gate Answers` section.
+Only proceed to the Workflow section below once all three questions have written answers.
+
+---
+
+**Q1 — Open Code Review Findings**
+
+List every finding from `features/<story-folder>/code-review.md` (Phase 8b) that was
+not fully resolved during Phase 8 implementation. For each finding, mark it as:
+
+- `RESOLVED in Phase 8` — brief note on how it was resolved
+- `DEFERRED to Phase 9` — this becomes a **mandatory work item** in Phase 9
+- `WONTFIX` — brief justification for not addressing it
+
+If Phase 8b was skipped (small-scope story), write:
+> "Phase 8b skipped — no code review findings."
+
+If `code-review.md` does not exist, write:
+> "code-review.md not found — Phase 8b was skipped or findings were addressed inline."
+
+---
+
+**Q2 — Test Coverage Assessment**
+
+Run `python -m pytest --cov=<module> --cov-report=term-missing` (or equivalent) and
+report:
+1. The current overall coverage percentage
+2. A list of every module or file **below 80% coverage** with its current percentage
+
+These below-80% modules become **mandatory coverage targets** for Phase 9 work.
+
+If coverage tooling is unavailable, write:
+> "Coverage tooling unavailable — manual audit:"
+> Then list the 3 most critical untested code paths by inspection.
+
+If this story has no backend Python code (e.g., content-only or frontend-only stories),
+write:
+> "Story is content-only / frontend-only — backend coverage not applicable."
+
+---
+
+**Q3 — Canon Gap Check**
+
+Did this story's implementation diverge from any `gc-data-v2/platform/` patterns, or
+introduce a new platform pattern not yet documented?
+
+- **If yes:** Run `/canon-backport STORY-NNNN` now. Paste the summary output here.
+  The canon-gap.yaml will be picked up by `/retro` to generate the gc-data-v2 companion
+  proposal at epic close.
+- **If no:** Write:
+  > "No canon gaps identified — implementation aligns with gc-data-v2/platform/ docs."
+- **If gc-data-v2 is not in scope for this story:** Write:
+  > "gc-data-v2 not in scope — this story does not touch data platform patterns."
+
+---
+
+**Gate decision:** Do NOT proceed to the Workflow below until all three questions have
+written answers in `refinement-report.md`. If any answer requires investigation
+(e.g., running coverage, scanning code review notes), do that investigation first.
+The 3-question gate takes ~5 minutes and prevents aimless polish.
+
+---
+
+## Workflow
+1. **Address Deferred Items:** Resolve findings from Phase 8b (Q1 DEFERRED list)
+2. **Polish Edge Cases:** Improve error handling, performance, UX
+3. **Increase Coverage:** Target 80%+ coverage on modules identified in Q2
+4. **Produce Refinement Report**
+
+## Outputs
+
+- `refinement-report.md` — Edge cases, performance, coverage increase, dependency health, cleanup
+  - Must include `## Phase 9 Gate Answers` section with Q1, Q2, Q3 answers
+- `.project` — Updated with Phase 9 complete
+
+## Advance
+- **Type:** confirm
+- **Next:** Done
